  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Rain will develop tonight and continue Thursday. \nFollowed by cold air arriving and supporting rain to mix with and \nchange to wet snow Thursday night through Saturday.\n\nA train of shortwave troughs will dive south, carving out the \nlongwave trough across the Great Lakes tonight through Thursday, \nwhere a deep broad closed low will take up residency across the \neastern CONUS and southeast Canada by Friday. Meanwhile at the \nsurface, an initial low will pass across the Ohio Valley tonight \nbefore giving way to a secondary coastal low off the coast of the \nDelmarva Thursday. This surface low will then move northeast off the \nNew England shores Thursday night through Friday night before \nreaching the Canadian Maritimes by Saturday afternoon.\n\nA combination of falling heights and increasing potential vorticity \nahead of the digging trough, along with increasing FGEN and \ndeformation north of the track of the surface low will resulting rain \nto develop from west to east this evening. Rain will then increase \nin coverage and intensity throughout the night due to saturating low \nlevels and increased synoptic forcing. \n\nWidespread rain will persist through Thursday as the aforementioned \nsurface low passes south of the region. A band of stronger \nprecipitation will develop across the Finger Lakes region due to a \nbroad area of FGEN and deformation persisting across northern PA and \ncentral NY. Temperatures will primarily remain above freezing \nThursday, supporting mainly all rain, however some colder air aloft \nwill begin to filter in across the North County Thursday afternoon \nwhere the higher terrain of the Tug Hill may see a change over to \nwest snow and have some minor slushy accumulation possible by the \nevening hours.\n\nAn inverted trough extending north from the developing surface low \nThursday night will allow for steady precip to continue across the \nMohawk and Hudson Valleys. Farther west, the deeper moisture and \nforcing will move away from Western NY, replaced by lake enhancement \nand upslope flow that will still support intermittent, light \nprecipitation. Temperatures aloft and at the surface will steadily \ncool overnight, resulting in a change to mainly wet snow for the \nhigher terrain in the evening, with the change to wet snow then \ngradually progressing to lower elevations overnight. The higher \nterrain of the Tug Hill and western Adirondacks may see a few inches \nof wet snow if the steadier precipitation holds through the night. \nFarther west, only expect very minor slushy accumulations across the \nhigher terrain of the Finger Lakes and western Southern Tier, with \nlittle or no accumulation for lower elevations.\n\nFriday through Saturday, chilly northerly flow will persist, as the \npool of coldest air aloft crosses the eastern Great Lakes Friday \nnight through Saturday morning. Moderate lake induced instability \nwill continue through the period, but short northerly fetch will \nkeep the lake response limited to relatively weak multiple bands and \nlake enhancement. Upslope flow will anchor the most persistent \nprecipitation across higher terrain, and also enhance QPF amounts to \nsome extent. Expect occasional rain and wet snow showers to continue \nthrough the period for lower elevations, with all wet snow across \nhigher terrain. Precip rates will be light most of the time and \nsurface temperatures marginal, so expect additional accumulations to \nbe minor and mainly focus across higher terrain.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will return by early next week, with \ntemperatures trending warmer by midweek.\n\nChilly cyclonic north flow will continue Sunday, although much of \nthe deeper synoptic scale moisture and forcing will have exited the \nregion. This may still support a few spotty light rain/wet snow \nshowers south of the lakes. \n\nDry weather will then return early next week as strong high pressure \nbuilds east across the Great Lakes. The dry weather will likely last \nmost of next week. It will remain chilly initially, then a warming \ntrend will develop once the surface high moves east of our longitude \nand height rises/warm advection increase aloft.",
